WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.303
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.303 --> 00:00:12.859
The greatest coach in the history of
professional sports Sir Alex Ferguson.

00:00:12.859 --> 00:00:22.431
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:22.431 --> 00:00:31.971
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:00:31.971 --> 00:00:34.265
&gt;&gt; You're looking very dapper tonight.

00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:35.050
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.183
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:37.638
&gt;&gt; My mother looks after me well.

00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:44.035
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Looks like we're the only
two who got dressed this evening.

00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:47.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So what would you do, Sir Alex,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.830
if some of this scruffy
mob here showed up and

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.619
were players, and
wanted to get on the Majesty United bus?

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:04.190
&gt;&gt; Well, first I was a coach, everyone
had to have a short haircut, all shaven.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:11.036
I don't know how managers allow players on
the bus with a beard, that's not for me.

00:01:11.036 --> 00:01:11.663
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:01:11.663 --> 00:01:13.641
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, whether

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.410
If I've not lost my strength,
if I would allow that.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:25.160
But what I did like to see was my team
coming to a ground with the United

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.240
blazer on, white shirt and a tie because
they're representing Manchester United.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.290
&gt;&gt; And what would you do if
they didn't show up like that?

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.530
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it's part of
the education you have to give them.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.160
The responsibility they have as
an Manchester United player.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.660
And it's just a discipline.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.390
And I think that was
a strong disciplined United.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:48.340
And it's still today.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:53.580
Even Louis van Gaal, all smart,
I know it's a the boys that are not

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.500
playing sit in the back of the director's
box, blazer and flannels on, I like that.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.560
That's for me.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.405
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry you guys with the beards.

00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:08.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You recommend a shave, would you?

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.220
So did you see the game on Saturday?

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.460
The Everton game.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.510
&gt;&gt; Yep. Yep. &gt;&gt; What'd you think?

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.610
&gt;&gt; Did very well.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.650
I thought it was going to be
a difficult game actually, and

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.860
I saw Everton did all
the strongest team out.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.862
You know, and I thought this is going to
be a difficult game because the last I

00:02:26.862 --> 00:02:31.271
think they've lost the last three years
there, but they won very comfortably.

00:02:31.271 --> 00:02:37.670
Complete control, the way they
change the system a little bit.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.950
I thought Evan couldn't handle it.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.900
So, I was pleased, because after,

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.550
the great test of any Manchester United
team is how you recover after defeats.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.300
There was three-nil with Arsenal,
a bad defeat.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.980
They come out at the next game, win.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.690
And that's the best way
to answer the critics and

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.510
also to show the resolve and
the determination to get over a defeat.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.974
Because it's not easy at Club United.

00:03:03.974 --> 00:03:08.195
When you lose you're front page,
when you win you're back page.

00:03:08.195 --> 00:03:11.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a difference.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.690
&gt;&gt; Better to be on the back page.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.930
So, there are, I think if I counted
correctly, there were 8 of the 18

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:24.465
players who showed up,
who were in the squad on Saturday.

00:03:24.465 --> 00:03:26.000
Were players that you had signed.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:34.960
How long does it take to knit everybody
else together into a really cohesive unit?

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.460
Is that a matter of years or
can you do it in a season?

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.733
&gt;&gt; Well, if you're talking with
the present court of players

00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:46.970
&gt;&gt; And five last season, five this year,

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:48.430
and that's difficult.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.892
Particularly, the players they brought
in were players from other countries.

00:03:51.892 --> 00:03:55.790
The division's very difficult,
really difficult.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.390
When we brought players in from abroad,
we always gave them first season,

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:03.680
forget it, second season, okay.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.400
But one or two excelled, surprised us
by doing really well at the beginning.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.660
In my time,
the thing that was different from me and

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.280
Louis Van Gaal of course
is that I had longevity.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.210
I was there 27 years.

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.950
So when I went to United at first,

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.090
my job was to build the foundation
at that football club.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.680
Because I think most managers,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.910
quite rightly, have to think about
a football team, the first team because

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:31.950
as a result industry they are to
make sure the first team does well.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.850
I never thought that way.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.075
I thought that rebuilding the youth in the
club so they would give me a foundation

00:04:38.075 --> 00:04:41.540
and I'd given them with the young
players coming through.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.250
My conviction was never going to change.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.730
I told the directors on day one,
that's exactly what I was going to do.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.240
And of course, yeah,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.216
I was concerned that the first team
[INAUDIBLE] second bout of the week.

00:04:53.216 --> 00:04:58.638
And I just took my time with that,
I wasn't in a hurry, most concentration

00:04:58.638 --> 00:05:03.391
was on scouting, trialing, and
coaching for the young people.

00:05:03.391 --> 00:05:03.928
&gt;&gt; Mm hm.

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:07.121
Now at the end of the Everton game,

00:05:07.121 --> 00:05:12.421
United are in third position
in English Premier League.

00:05:12.421 --> 00:05:14.140
It's the end of October and

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:19.462
obviously everybody here is connected to
the business school in one way or another.

00:05:19.462 --> 00:05:23.480
And an important thing in
business is setting expectations.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:28.710
And how would you,
were you managing United today,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.530
knowing that there's a long part
of the season still to come?

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.920
You still gotta play Christmas,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.310
you gotta get, you're in the other
different cups and championships.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.870
How do you go about setting
the expectation of where you would want

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:50.330
the club to wind up the end of the season
knowing your in third position today.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:55.796
&gt;&gt; Well I think the definition,
you take my job Aberdeen.

00:05:55.796 --> 00:05:58.120
Aberdeen's an awful Scotland.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:04.130
Cut off from the central bell with
a mean stream of footballers.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:08.786
I had to build an expectation,
to create an expectation for the players.

00:06:08.786 --> 00:06:12.180
Whereas in United you have to
live with the expectations for

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.160
every player that comes through that club.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.020
Even after Saturday's game,

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.890
every player on that team has
to weather the expectation.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.620
So, the expectation is to win.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:26.170
Absolute, whether it's a European cup or
a cup.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:27.390
They all have to win.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:28.590
That's the mentality they have.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.960
The mindset is the winning mindset.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.380
There's no question about that.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:37.234
So, I never said to the press, we've got
to win the European cup, we've got to the.

00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:37.734
I've never said that.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.750
Every time at press conference,
well, I hope we win something.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:45.410
I wouldn't want to get carried away and
give them a headline.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:47.950
But, deep down, win every game.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:48.910
That was the mentality.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.915
I never expected to lose a game, ever.

00:06:50.915 --> 00:06:51.970
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:56.258
Would you be talking to the players at
this juncture about the possibility of

00:06:56.258 --> 00:06:58.007
winning the league this year?

00:06:58.007 --> 00:06:58.593
&gt;&gt; No.

00:06:58.593 --> 00:07:06.090
&gt;&gt; And when would you start privately
calculating whether or not you thought.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.390
&gt;&gt; You won't believe this if I tell you.

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:11.120
Every begin of January.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.010
I used to get all opponents games and

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:18.510
predict the point that we
want to get against us.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.510
And I was never far wrong.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.170
Never far wrong.

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:23.690
Even to the point that,

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:29.010
I knew we'd maybe have to make three
points up on one of our main opponents.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.250
I was pretty accurate in that.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:31.160
And I did this every year.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:34.140
And so that, add my win.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.150
Sort of a challenge.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:41.220
&gt;&gt; Would you sit down with each of
the top clubs or the whole league?

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.080
&gt;&gt; Just the top clubs, only the four ones.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.468
I will know by January 1st who our
main challenges are going to be.

00:07:47.468 --> 00:07:52.552
And then I used to do that, I kept to
myself, I didn't share it with anyone.

00:07:52.552 --> 00:07:54.471
I was never far wrong, That's good.

00:07:54.471 --> 00:07:56.932
&gt;&gt; I knew even the years we lost it.

00:07:56.932 --> 00:08:02.740
I knew that dangers we had
in terms of challenges.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.225
&gt;&gt; You're pretty accurate about,
say in January, about the number of points

00:08:06.225 --> 00:08:08.740
that United itself would have
at the end of the season.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Let me change the topic a little bit,

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:19.030
to two things that again are germane
to every sort of business.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:23.160
One is assessing and judging talents and
another is discipline.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.180
So, let me take you back.

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.220
It's 1957.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.455
There is a young man growing sideburns.

00:08:32.455 --> 00:08:36.811
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Alex Ferguson who's

00:08:36.811 --> 00:08:40.344
turning out in his first seasons for

00:08:40.344 --> 00:08:45.535
a club in Scotland in
Glasgow called Queen's Park.

00:08:45.535 --> 00:08:48.495
How would you assess
the talent of that player?

00:08:48.495 --> 00:08:53.335
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:53.335 --> 00:08:57.257
&gt;&gt; Well, outstanding comes to mind.

00:08:57.257 --> 00:09:01.783
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, well, I probably

00:09:01.783 --> 00:09:06.750
was one of the few players that played for
Glasgow Skills, Scotland Skills,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:11.210
Scotland Youth, Scottish Amateur,
and the full Scottish team.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.618
But I wasn't an outstanding player,
I was a goal scorer.

00:09:13.618 --> 00:09:21.490
In fact, at United we used to get into
the video analysis room most mornings.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:25.100
And this morning our Goalkeeping
coach Eric Steele says,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.630
I was just pulling up
your goalscoring record.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:28.860
He said that's pretty good, that.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.760
179 goals in 300 games or
something like that.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.490
And I said, that's only league goals,
I said where are my cup goals?

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.030
So, I said, well,
I'll try and get them out.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.110
We couldn't get them.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.450
We couldn't find any cup goals.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.860
I don't know why, but
No, I was a goal scorer,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.000
and had a great career without
actually winning anything.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:51.210
I went to Rangers about the time when
Celtic were completely dominant.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.767
They'd just won the European Cup
under Jock Stein, and

00:09:54.767 --> 00:09:56.740
they were the fantastic team.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.050
The were a devil to beat at that time.

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:00.580
But I enjoyed it, I had a good career.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.733
The thing about playing is that
that is the best time of your life,

00:10:04.733 --> 00:10:09.958
which relates to how I would never ask any
of my players to retire to become a coach.

00:10:09.958 --> 00:10:14.240
I encouraged them to take their badges.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.800
When I went to fulltime at football,
because I was an engineer.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:20.740
I was a tool maker until I was 22,
was part-time at football.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:21.700
I made my mind up.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.847
I was not going back to Engineering.

00:10:23.847 --> 00:10:26.210
So I took all my coaching badges,
I prepared to stay in the game.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.766
Like everyone should do,
if you want to do something, prepare.

00:10:29.766 --> 00:10:33.735
Whether it's through study, or
like I did, take your coaching badges,

00:10:33.735 --> 00:10:35.970
I think that's an important issue.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:40.110
&gt;&gt; And how well behaved on the field
was that young player Alex Ferguson?

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.720
&gt;&gt; You're getting this from my son Jason.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:44.487
Had a bad record.

00:10:44.487 --> 00:10:46.228
&gt;&gt; Pardon?

00:10:46.228 --> 00:10:47.204
&gt;&gt; Had a bad record.

00:10:47.204 --> 00:10:53.102
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was sent off eight times

00:10:53.102 --> 00:10:54.239
&gt;&gt; Pardon?

00:10:54.239 --> 00:10:56.422
&gt;&gt; I know, I know.

00:10:56.422 --> 00:10:58.384
But I was misunderstood.

00:10:58.384 --> 00:11:02.030
[LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Do you remember some

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:11.353
of the incidents about
why you were sent off?

00:11:11.353 --> 00:11:13.465
&gt;&gt; Every one of them.

00:11:13.465 --> 00:11:14.068
Yeah.

00:11:14.068 --> 00:11:17.374
Retaliation was mostly.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:19.960
Or maybe a late tackle or

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:24.278
something, or an elbow in someone's jaw or
something like that.

00:11:24.278 --> 00:11:27.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I had a funny running style.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.400
I run with my elbows like this.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.422
So, I was always getting into trouble.

00:11:32.422 --> 00:11:33.851
[LAUGH] Matt's laughing here.

00:11:33.851 --> 00:11:36.716
I was always getting into trouble
because of my elbows, yeah.

00:11:36.716 --> 00:11:38.237
&gt;&gt; The elbows.

00:11:38.237 --> 00:11:38.737
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:47.330
So let's talk about judging other
talent several decades later.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:50.790
I'm going to pick two players,
David Beckham and

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.790
another one we'll come to in a moment.

00:11:54.018 --> 00:11:58.860
So, how did you first come across
Beckham and how old was he?

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.460
And what did he seem like as
a player when you first saw him?

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:06.900
&gt;&gt; Well, David came to know
us through a scout in London,

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.250
from the same area as David.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.730
He was a headmaster at school,
Malcolm Fidgen.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:21.010
And he put David on the radar, but
the real impact came from Bobby Charlton.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:27.650
Bobby had soccer schools at the time,
and David had won a placement for

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:30.530
Bobby's soccer school in Barcelona.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:34.550
And Bobby came back and
said I've seen a kid, you need.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.090
So I flagged it up with the chief scout.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.890
He said, of course,
he's coming next month.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:41.040
Just by coincidence.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.100
And that was a start, he would be
eleven years of age at the time.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:49.350
He was a little thin boy,
he had no height, no physique whatsoever.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:53.090
But he had this wonderful talent,
in terms of control of the ball,

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:56.638
striking the ball was really his forte.

00:12:56.638 --> 00:12:59.070
And, his parents were United fans.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:04.750
His grandfather was a Tottenham fan, but
he and his parents were United fans.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.060
So he used to come to the game.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.830
After we got the contact with him,

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:11.840
we invited him to the games
every time we were in London.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:12.750
And, in fact,

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:16.710
he was a a ball boy in the West Ham
game which is this area of London.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:21.330
And he was a certainty to come to United.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.090
He trained at Tottenham for a while.

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:27.330
I think he trained somewhere else,
maybe Fulham or something like that.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.040
But he was always destined
to come to United because he

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.980
wanted to come to United.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:36.146
And, in that class of 92, who were
the team to win the Youth Cup, David never

00:13:36.146 --> 00:13:40.870
got on the team until the semi-finals
because he was just a little boy.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:48.111
Then, within months,
[SOUND] six foot, still thin,

00:13:48.111 --> 00:13:52.626
no confirmation in his body whatsoever.

00:13:52.626 --> 00:13:55.176
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, he was a skinny boy.

00:13:55.176 --> 00:13:56.778
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then,

00:13:56.778 --> 00:14:00.902
That group with the same desires,
the seven or

00:14:00.902 --> 00:14:06.294
eight of them, they talk about
the ones that are well known,

00:14:06.294 --> 00:14:11.880
Giggs, Scholes, Butt,
the two Nevilles and David.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.690
But we had two other players would've been
great players, but their careers were cut

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:19.920
by injury, Chris Casper and
Ben Thornley, they were outstanding.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.385
But they all practiced.

00:14:22.385 --> 00:14:26.340
&gt;&gt; Give a sense of how
hard these guys worked.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:28.320
&gt;&gt; Well, the training would
start 10 o'clock to 12 o'clock.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.140
The other ones, they would be out in
the afternoon practicing all the time, or

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.060
with the youth coach in terms
of their general training.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:42.270
And for David, who lived in accommodation
with Mrs. Gosling with several other boys,

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:48.190
he would go into the school of at
nighttime and help the coaches.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:49.570
With the young kids.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:54.150
So his desires were strong, and
also he was a practicer, he'd practice,

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.980
practice, practice.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:02.160
And I think that the advice
I'd give to any young kid is,

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.340
anyone can play a game of football,
anyone.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:07.000
But practice makes you a real footballer.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.980
You see games on a Sunday.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.200
Pub games.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.170
Oh, love a game of football.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:17.360
The real players had
a practice ethic about them.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.620
I remember being at lunch in Glasgow many,
many years ago,

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:24.090
and Gary Player was the guest.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:24.750
&gt;&gt; The golfer?
&gt;&gt; The golfer.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:31.420
And they asked him that question, why
do you keep practicing from the bunker?

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.280
Because he was famous for,
you know, the bunkers.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:37.640
I know, I'm going to be in
the bunker at one time in the game,

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.340
maybe twice in the game, and
I've got to put it in the hole.

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.740
That's why I practiced, and
practiced, and practiced.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:47.758
That's a great example of what you
have to do to be a top player.

00:15:47.758 --> 00:15:50.252
And David and
all these young lads did that same.

00:15:50.252 --> 00:15:54.007
And all the young kids
at United practice well.

00:15:54.007 --> 00:15:57.977
&gt;&gt; Different Well known
name Christiano Ronaldo,

00:15:57.977 --> 00:16:01.666
how old was he when you
first came across him?

00:16:01.666 --> 00:16:02.730
&gt;&gt; 17 he was.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.030
&gt;&gt; And
how did he first tip your radar screen?

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:14.450
&gt;&gt; That's a great story because my
assistant, of course is a Portuguese.

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:17.180
And he thought it'd be a good idea for
us to have an association,

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:21.260
a relationship with the sport in Lisbon,
because he'd been there as a kid, and

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:22.960
he was from Lisbon.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:28.710
And he set it up, and what we're
doing then was sharing coaching.

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:32.260
So we would send coaches over there,
and they would send coaches to us.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:36.750
And Jim Ryan, who was head of youth
department at the time, youth scouting.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:40.050
He came back, he says,
well I've seen a player.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:44.211
And he was playing center for
youth team at the time.

00:16:44.211 --> 00:16:52.220
So I says to it would be a good idea if
maybe we'd build a relationship here.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:56.130
And he spoke to the president,
and the deal was

00:16:56.130 --> 00:17:01.040
that we couldn't get him the next year,
but we could get them two years from then.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:07.040
Then [INAUDIBLE] left me the next year,
we'd promised

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:11.205
through the association with him, the
relationship, we'd offer the new stadium.

00:17:11.205 --> 00:17:13.080
&gt;&gt; This is at Sporting [INAUDIBLE]?

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:14.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Sporting, that's correct.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:22.170
And we flew from the States, from here,
to Lisbon and played the game.

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:26.390
And Christiano's playing outside
left against John O'Shea,

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:31.670
and the poor soul's got tortured blood.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.940
Honestly, he is absolutely annihilated.

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:36.100
And I'm up there going, John!

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.300
Get up and get some.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:38.145
He says, I can't.

00:17:38.145 --> 00:17:42.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I get my kit manager.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.767
I says,
go up there to the directors box and

00:17:44.767 --> 00:17:47.422
get Peter Kenyon down
right away at half time.

00:17:47.422 --> 00:17:48.662
So Peter Kenyon comes down.

00:17:48.662 --> 00:17:50.220
I says, we're not leaving here.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.335
&gt;&gt; Peter Kenyon was the then-
&gt;&gt; Chief executive.

00:17:51.335 --> 00:17:53.080
&gt;&gt; Chief executive of United.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:54.270
&gt;&gt; At that time, yeah.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:58.640
And I says, we're not going to go and
wave in here that you're playing.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:03.210
So then we got a little room after
the game with the president of Lisbon,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:09.170
his agent, his lawyer and the boy.

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:13.350
I said I want you to come
back with us to Manchester.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:14.730
Now that's a test.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.780
If he doesn't want to come,
you know he doesn't want to join you.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.540
He says, yeah I'd love to come but
I want my mother.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:23.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:25.120
&gt;&gt; So I said, great.

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:30.180
Next morning his mother, his sister,
his brother, the whole trip come out.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:34.670
And I said to him, I promise you
one thing, you'll get the best

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:38.720
education you could possibly get here,
but you won't play every week.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.190
And he looked at him that.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:43.182
He was impressed.

00:18:43.182 --> 00:18:45.620
He didn't expect that.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:50.600
I says, the Premier League's
a very difficult league

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.990
and you may play substitute sometime,
you play an odd game,

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:58.859
you may in the League Cup which we
do have a lot of young players.

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:02.600
And, he said, but
when do I get in the first team?

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:07.430
Well, if you get to a level that's
Manchester United, I can't stop you.

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:08.190
You pick yourself.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:12.940
And he played his first game, he was sub
in the first game of the season against

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:16.500
Bolton and he came on at half time and
the crowd went absolute mad with them.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:17.990
He was absolutely fantastic.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:22.920
He had courage to take the ball,
the first rate back of

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:27.820
first minute, he come on the second half,
he got them and says, give me the ball.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:30.085
And the great players
have gotten this courage.

00:19:30.085 --> 00:19:31.020
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:31.985
&gt;&gt; Nothing will daunt them.

00:19:31.985 --> 00:19:34.280
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; They're not afraid of anything.

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:35.386
And Christiano had that.

00:19:35.386 --> 00:19:36.978
&gt;&gt; And how,
when he first came to Manchester,

00:19:36.978 --> 00:19:39.051
I think he came with his mother and
his mother's sister.

00:19:39.051 --> 00:19:40.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:46.920
How disciplined was he in his sort
of daily ritual at that point?

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:50.480
&gt;&gt; He was the best
practicer I've ever known.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:57.376
But he had these what you would
call Latin American faults.

00:19:57.376 --> 00:20:02.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You can get arrested for

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:04.467
saying that in the state of California.

00:20:04.467 --> 00:20:08.040
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it's a simple fault,

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:12.680
which can be eradicated, if the boy's
got the right desire and discipline.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.300
And listen to us.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:16.400
And he was diving as a young kid.

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.160
&gt;&gt; Diving to try and get a penalty.

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:19.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah correct.

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:22.970
The players were great with him.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:27.350
Every time he'd go down and scream, the
players would go an scream at him back.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.505
Get up you, and all that.

00:20:29.505 --> 00:20:32.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I used to get at him all the time.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:37.030
And the unfortunate thing for Ronaldo
because he was such a great player.

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:41.943
The press never left him alone in that
situation because After about a year,

00:20:41.943 --> 00:20:44.336
year and a half, it never happened.

00:20:44.336 --> 00:20:48.648
But any time we've failed,
someone all knowing and

00:20:48.648 --> 00:20:51.884
curious will say, oh yeah, he dives.

00:20:51.884 --> 00:20:56.727
But no, it's not true, because [INAUDIBLE]
against defenders, all you need

00:20:56.727 --> 00:21:01.226
to do is nudge him a little bit and
the [INAUDIBLE] scores and they go down.

00:21:01.226 --> 00:21:04.665
But it wasn't, [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:04.665 --> 00:21:06.549
&gt;&gt; Did the criticism get to him at all?

00:21:06.549 --> 00:21:08.750
&gt;&gt; No no.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.340
I remember he come off of that period,

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:16.458
at the season [INAUDIBLE]
off,

00:21:16.458 --> 00:21:18.410
and he said he wasn't going to come back.

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:23.750
And I flew out to Portugal and I said
look, he wanted to go to Real Madrid.

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:27.770
It was always his lifetime
ambition to do that.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:35.170
And I said, look, you give me a year and
you go on with my blessing to Real Madrid.

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:36.630
He was fantastic.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:38.070
But the first game was at Charlton.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:45.590
And I sat in the director's box, and
there's this guy down in front of me.

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:50.450
He's up and he's giving them so
much, the cursing, the language.

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:54.420
Then about five minutes from the half
time, he got the ball, he beat three men,

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:56.333
cut in, hit the underside of the bar.

00:21:56.333 --> 00:21:58.794
The guy never got up again.

00:21:58.794 --> 00:22:00.810
That wasn't finished.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:02.766
He had the courage to do all that.

00:22:02.766 --> 00:22:08.330
&gt;&gt; And Christiano today,
considerable many years later now,

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:11.030
does he still have the same
sort of drive and work ethic?

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:14.140
&gt;&gt; I've never known anyone like him,
honestly.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:17.120
I was at his house for
dinner a few month ago.

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:20.660
And his services made for the best player
in the world, no doubt about that.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:21.450
&gt;&gt; How's that?

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:25.770
&gt;&gt; The gymnasium's probably about half
the the size of this, this size here.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.730
He's got everything in it.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:31.162
He's got two pools,
he's got a hot pool and a cold pool.

00:22:31.162 --> 00:22:34.665
He's got an atrium at minus 160 degrees.

00:22:34.665 --> 00:22:37.720
He goes in there for
ten minutes after every game.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.458
It's just unbelievable.

00:22:40.458 --> 00:22:44.705
And I says to him, he says to me,
I'm going to retire at 35.

00:22:44.705 --> 00:22:46.295
I said you don't retire at 35.

00:22:46.295 --> 00:22:48.470
You can play til you're 40, easily.

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:49.940
Ryan Giggs has played to 41.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:53.540
Says no, no, I'm doing that, and
I'm working on making sure I'm

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:56.270
three kilograms under my best
weight the last five years.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:59.298
But I went, brilliant.

00:22:59.298 --> 00:23:00.246
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:23:00.246 --> 00:23:01.620
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.660
Now, with all these people,
with all players of incredible caliber,

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:10.510
Eckermann, Aldove,
the others that you had, how do,

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:15.140
what were the tools that you
used to maintain discipline?

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:21.930
&gt;&gt; Well, I think that the most important
thing is to be consistent, who you are and

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:23.340
don't change.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.200
And I never changed, I didn't use

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:33.200
discipline as a weapon against players.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:37.540
They would be disciplined if they
stepped out of line for certain reasons,

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:41.820
maybe a silly sending off or
a misbehaving outside the ground.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:46.445
But I never used it as
a continual weapon against them.

00:23:46.445 --> 00:23:47.466
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:23:47.466 --> 00:23:48.429
&gt;&gt; You got disciplined.

00:23:48.429 --> 00:23:49.426
It's over.

00:23:49.426 --> 00:23:50.347
We move on.

00:23:50.347 --> 00:23:52.676
And they all accept that, eventually.

00:23:52.676 --> 00:23:55.135
They.
They understand who you are.

00:23:55.135 --> 00:23:59.040
That's the most important thing, because
that's the essence of being a leader.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:23:59.880
They know who you are.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:03.760
You're consistent,
they trust that you're right, and

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:08.660
this one is an important aspect of
managing them, there's no question.

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:12.099
But technically, you're dealing
with very well paid players.

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:16.840
If you've got a discipline that's
different with different people,

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:18.720
you're going to wrong way.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.260
So, my dicipline is always the same for
everyone.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:23.120
I never treated anyone any different.

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:27.060
&gt;&gt; And what was the sort of
discipline that the players dreaded?

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:32.600
Was it the fine, or
the threat of being transferred or

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.630
being put in the stands to watch a game.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:39.686
&gt;&gt; I think every player wants to play in.

00:24:39.686 --> 00:24:41.950
Even though we had no indication of it,

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.780
disappointed player by
being in the stands.

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:47.850
But the other aspect of
putting players in the stands

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.800
is because you have a score to 24.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:51.970
And you have to pick 1 11.

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:54.580
Now that's difficult.

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:58.470
But what I used to do was,
because my own experience as a player,

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.330
I was told 50 minutes from a kick-off in
the Scottish Cup final I wasn't playing.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:06.030
I was the top goalscorer and
there was no subs at the time.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:11.220
So that was always in my mind about how
to treat players if they're not playing.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:14.070
So I used to call them in,
individual with all of them, and

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.000
explain to them why they weren't playing.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:22.810
It was very easy as time went on
because they knew with a squad of 24,

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:26.980
it's not the 11 that win the league, it's
the 24 that win the league or the cup.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.110
No question about that.

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:33.660
And the important part to do that is to
trust them to whatever team you play.

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:37.370
And make them feel that
they have contributed

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:39.490
like the best players in winning.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:46.160
&gt;&gt; And Sir Alex, how did you ensure
that even for the bigger stars they all

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:51.760
understood that they were part of,
not the, but part of a team?

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:57.470
&gt;&gt; Well I think the best players,
the ones that want to win more.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:02.010
The best players who've got that ego,
a win is important to them.

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:03.020
So I enjoyed that.

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.170
I enjoyed players with that confidence.

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:10.100
&gt;&gt; But how did you make sure that none
of them began to think that they were

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.830
bigger than United in themselves?

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:14.020
&gt;&gt; Well the message is,
to prove they're the best,

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.760
they have to work as hard as everyone
else and even more than other ones.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:19.410
And that was the best
you'd always give to them.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.750
The evidence is always
on the football field.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:26.000
The best player can let themselves down,

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:30.453
by not living up to expectation,
his own expectation in fact.

00:26:30.453 --> 00:26:33.164
[SOUND] Oops!

00:26:33.164 --> 00:26:34.020
That's gone.

00:26:35.482 --> 00:26:41.425
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I had no great worries with that.

00:26:41.425 --> 00:26:43.650
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:46.630
&gt;&gt; The fight had been there such
a long time of course helps, but

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:52.640
also I think that players who come to
United, that club is such a great club.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.340
They don't want to miss the bus.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:57.230
They want to go on the bus all the time.

00:26:57.230 --> 00:26:59.070
And I think that was very important.

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:04.480
&gt;&gt; Now, how do you, let's switch the topic
a little bit and talk about compensations.

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:08.430
Some of these players are getting paid
a large amount of money, obviously.

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:10.420
How do managers cope?

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:17.290
Not you, because you were among the
highest compensated in the Premier League.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:23.250
But there are some clubs where the
managers are paid less than their players.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:30.930
How do they ever have a chance of
establishing authority in those clubs?

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:35.870
&gt;&gt; I think in most cases I was maybe a
little bit different because I always felt

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:39.080
that being the manager of
Manchester United I should be the highest

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:39.695
paid person.

00:27:39.695 --> 00:27:42.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah but

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:47.270
it's important because the responsibility
of managing that club is huge.

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:49.830
I'm there more hours than any of them.

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:52.060
I've got the biggest responsibility.

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:53.790
So I feel that should
be always recognized.

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:55.690
Not always years, by the way.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:59.535
Took a long time to agree with me.

00:27:59.535 --> 00:28:01.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:06.440
I think from other managers' point of
view, I think they expect that the very,

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.830
very top player is going to be paid more.

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:11.540
I have no issue at all with what
players get paid, the top players.

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:12.590
You know why?

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:14.100
I think if you look at golfers.

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:14.810
&gt;&gt; For sure.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.135
&gt;&gt; American footballers,
basketball players.

00:28:17.135 --> 00:28:19.705
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Head funded masters.

00:28:19.705 --> 00:28:22.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly.

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:24.902
Or potentially your
wealthy old men are there.

00:28:24.902 --> 00:28:28.543
But then, I have no issue with that
because they're bringing 75,000 people

00:28:28.543 --> 00:28:29.540
when they all effort.

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:30.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:33.370
Yeah, yeah.

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:37.900
What about saying good bye to players,
whether they were 16, 17,

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:42.160
18 year olds you didn't
think were going to make it?

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:46.162
And then let's talk about them and then I
wanted to talk about some of the players

00:28:46.162 --> 00:28:48.862
who'd done well by you that
you had to say good bye to.

00:28:48.862 --> 00:28:50.202
What about that case?

00:28:50.202 --> 00:28:51.773
&gt;&gt; Well the most difficult task for

00:28:51.773 --> 00:28:55.780
any manager is telling a young kid
he's not going to make it United.

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:59.630
When we bring a boy to the club,
we speak to the parents and

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:02.390
we say, we always say the same.

00:29:02.390 --> 00:29:06.460
We hope your boy plays in front
of 75,000 people, if not,

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:08.860
we're confident we can get
him a career in the game.

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:13.730
Even to this day,
there will be about 90 players in England,

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:19.450
Scotland, Germany all started
a career as a Manchester native.

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:24.190
And that's been like that for
10 to 15 years.

00:29:24.190 --> 00:29:26.890
Very difficult to tell them that.

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.520
I did this stupid thing where
something as a young coach.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:30.830
I was filmed.

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:37.100
I was terrified to tell a young boy he was
not you know, going to be good enough.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.600
So I had this idea bring
the five of them in.

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:42.690
&gt;&gt; Five young boys together?

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:43.320
&gt;&gt; And I told them.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:44.440
One of them started crying.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:46.180
Oh I never.

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:48.405
That was that.
Finished.

00:29:48.405 --> 00:29:49.190
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:50.370
&gt;&gt; It was a difficult time.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:51.529
It was not easy.

00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:58.550
&gt;&gt; And what about some of the older
players who had done very well by you and

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.750
then you had to break the news
to them that they were done?

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:06.404
&gt;&gt; That's equally hard because you
know I'd become a father to them, and

00:30:06.404 --> 00:30:09.908
it's such a family business
[INAUDIBLE] anyway that.

00:30:11.914 --> 00:30:17.110
For instance a team on 94 worked
with us for quite a few years.

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:24.680
&gt;From round about 91, right through to the
1967, with Dennis Ober and Steve Bruce.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.780
Brian Robson, Gar Powester.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:30.416
And they'd love to play to the 90.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:34.570
But they don't actually
realize they get older.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:38.710
I'm the one that recognizes it because
I see it on the football field.

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:45.260
But what I did with them,
was made sure we give them free transfers.

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:52.130
So they made the free bob, Dennis went
to Steve Bruce went to Birmingham.

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:54.160
Bryan Robson actually became
a manager at Middlesbrough.

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:59.510
And Gary Paulsen went back
to Middlesbrough with him.

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:00.710
So [INAUDIBLE] with Paul Parker.

00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:01.826
We gave him a free transfer.

00:31:01.826 --> 00:31:03.490
[INAUDIBLE] A free transfer.

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:05.770
They've got clubs, West Brom.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.000
Paul went to Fulham.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.170
So, we did well by them.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:13.900
And recognition for the service they give
us, and I think that was real important.

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:18.970
Money doesn't come into it when
the guy's giving you ten years.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:20.380
So I think it's the best
way you could do it.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:22.150
And the only thing you can do,

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:25.020
and it definitely works,
is to be honest with them.

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:28.310
That's the only thing that works.

00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:31.070
You don't want to go take these guys on.

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:33.260
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Professionals,

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:35.250
who have been great to you.

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:36.340
&gt;&gt; Different topic.

00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:40.269
What about, again, for a lot of people
setting out on lives in management and

00:31:40.269 --> 00:31:41.005
everything.

00:31:41.005 --> 00:31:46.721
What are some of the things that you
wished you had known when you were 30 or

00:31:46.721 --> 00:31:52.182
32, just setting off on your lifetime
of management up in Scotland?

00:31:52.182 --> 00:31:53.508
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I got this at Harvard.

00:31:53.508 --> 00:31:57.507
This is I belted the question,
had to think about it for a while.

00:31:57.507 --> 00:32:02.039
And the easiest one I think
about is communication,

00:32:02.039 --> 00:32:05.130
recognition, absolutely vital.

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:09.490
When I was 32, I wanted to rule the world.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:14.420
I was a young manager at Most
players are playing at 32, but

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:19.350
I had a groin injury and
I gave up to go into management.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:21.440
&gt;&gt; It wasn't the elbows that were?

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.338
&gt;&gt; No, there was, they were polished.

00:32:23.338 --> 00:32:29.385
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I was holding the grass feed for

00:32:29.385 --> 00:32:34.760
the groundsmen, and pies for
the the director's room.

00:32:34.760 --> 00:32:37.330
The programs on Saturday.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:43.640
And, you know,
you forget to manage up the way.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.750
When the chairmen went to go full time,
you know when that became-

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:48.710
&gt;&gt; Mmhmmm, right.

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:50.550
Right.
&gt;&gt; I didn't realize I still

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:51.655
carried on the way I did.

00:32:51.655 --> 00:32:52.340
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:53.701
&gt;&gt; Of course he can sack you.

00:32:53.701 --> 00:32:56.440
Which he did for a [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; So let's talk about the two things.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:58.170
One, how did you learn to delegate?

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:03.700
&gt;&gt; I think,
the natural thing about delegation was,

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:07.685
I wasn't properly recognizing
until I got to about maybe 60.

00:33:07.685 --> 00:33:08.950
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:10.000
&gt;&gt; And then I realized, is well,

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:13.660
if I'm going to keep my energy,
I've got to do it.

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:15.850
And then you have to
give the trust to that.

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.820
By that time anyway I
trusted all my staff anyway.

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:22.630
It was just a natural thing
that when you've got energy and

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:27.190
you've done something for so long,
you don't think it's ever going to change.

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:29.990
But, of course, age changes you.

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:32.130
And then I delegated far better.

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:35.370
I delegated a lot actually after that.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:41.120
The thing of it energy and anyone
listening to this, is very important.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:45.950
When you stop, where you've add to the
united, we'll see at 43, 44 years of age.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:50.190
The seething energy, the pure seething
energy, you see it burns over you.

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:55.585
But when you get to the 60s,
they still expect to see it.

00:33:55.585 --> 00:33:57.310
&gt;&gt; Mh-hm.

00:33:57.310 --> 00:34:01.890
&gt;&gt; So I had to look at myself in
terms of energy, that I was a select.

00:34:01.890 --> 00:34:05.910
because when I was a young manager,
four or five years, no problem.

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:10.493
When I got to my 60s,
I needed six or seven hours.

00:34:10.493 --> 00:34:12.810
So you go to bed earlier.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:16.960
You work at your diet better,
after the game at home.

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:21.668
I never, very very seldom and ate.

00:34:21.668 --> 00:34:27.550
So that added examine myself of my energy
because people want to see the energy.

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:30.660
A very important aspect to it, you know?

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:35.249
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the second aspect of that
was what you just alluded to, during,

00:34:35.249 --> 00:34:37.331
coming a cropper with an owner.

00:34:37.331 --> 00:34:37.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:41.890
&gt;&gt; What's the advice you give managers
these days these days when they call

00:34:41.890 --> 00:34:46.090
you about the relationship that
they should have with the owner of

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:47.226
the club that they work for?

00:34:47.226 --> 00:34:47.994
&gt;&gt; You've got to find a way.

00:34:47.994 --> 00:34:49.740
It doesn't matter what you think of them.

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:53.970
You've got to find out whether that
can be a good harmony, going together.

00:34:53.970 --> 00:34:59.120
And also to agree that you're
both going the same direction.

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:04.000
I think that, even if you find a way
of actually getting on with them, and

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:07.814
they're going that way and you're
going that way, it still doesn't work.

00:35:07.814 --> 00:35:11.590
So you have to find out
that we have agreed

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:14.890
were the direction the club is going,
and the job you're doing.

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:20.060
Now that I think of it, reckon it's
communication, is a really important part

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:25.770
too, that you must recognize
that people are working for you.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:29.270
Knowing their names, saying good
morning to them in the morning.

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:32.526
I had a manager, as a player,
he couldn't say good morning to you.

00:35:32.526 --> 00:35:33.363
He'd walk by you.

00:35:33.363 --> 00:35:34.150
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:37.805
&gt;&gt; Now that cold be racket conference,
could be anything.

00:35:37.805 --> 00:35:38.510
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:40.380
&gt;&gt; But you have to recognize
that people work for you.

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:41.360
&gt;&gt; Right, right.

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:45.880
&gt;&gt; And every time you win a cup or
trophy, every man by that staff or

00:35:45.880 --> 00:35:46.620
in that canteen.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:54.080
The laundry girls, the canteen staff,
the groundsman, the champion out,

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:58.770
because to see a trophy as much as
the players, they became a team that way.

00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:02.318
&gt;&gt; Now,
did you enjoy the celebrations yourself?

00:36:02.318 --> 00:36:05.010
&gt;&gt; No, no no.

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:08.773
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because any game lasts for

00:36:08.773 --> 00:36:10.852
me about half an hour.

00:36:10.852 --> 00:36:13.324
And I'm thinking about tomorrow.

00:36:13.324 --> 00:36:15.737
And I was just-
&gt;&gt; Even after 1999,

00:36:15.737 --> 00:36:17.860
the famous treble season?

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:19.850
&gt;&gt; After the dinner I went to bed.

00:36:19.850 --> 00:36:21.170
After the club, I went to my bed.

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:24.436
&gt;&gt; Do you wake the next
morning in a bad mood, or?

00:36:24.436 --> 00:36:28.280
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was exhausted.

00:36:28.280 --> 00:36:33.765
Actually, when the team are getting
presented with the cup I went for a walk.

00:36:33.765 --> 00:36:35.580
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:41.080
&gt;&gt; And the bus one has all these alleys
and little corridors and all of this and

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:45.560
I'm walking through them all and bump
into this friend of mine from Aberdeen.

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:47.960
He couldn't take either.

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.330
He was wanting the,
heard from the manager.

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:53.490
And we're working through
all these alleys,

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:55.260
and then we see this buggy coming up.

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:58.660
And it's a guy who's looking for
me, he says,

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:00.760
you gotta go to the press conference.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:04.265
This is about half an hour after the game.

00:37:04.265 --> 00:37:08.850
I can celebrate for half an hour,
then it was [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:12.170
&gt;&gt; Right,
how did you avoid complacency and

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:16.430
getting complacent and-
&gt;&gt; Well it's a disease you know.

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:20.740
Every time at half time if we win a game
has come up, it comes up all the time.

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:23.140
you know there's nothing you can do
about it when you get complacent.

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:29.880
I mean a few occasions its never one
in the same connect with United because

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:35.180
their human beings but we kept reminding
them when half the time your willing.

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:37.906
Complacency, It's a disease.

00:37:37.906 --> 00:37:39.984
The Ryder Cup.

00:37:39.984 --> 00:37:43.920
This is the message I gave to the team
in the Ryder Cup that were in Europe-

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:44.870
&gt;&gt; The British team?

00:37:44.870 --> 00:37:46.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:51.405
And, I told them, I said you think
about Medinah two years before.

00:37:51.405 --> 00:37:52.910
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:56.350
&gt;&gt; And, the ten-six on a Saturday night.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:59.386
They're winning ten-six, America.

00:37:59.386 --> 00:38:01.120
And I'm sitting there in the dinner.

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:05.880
And they're promising each other,
when we win these four and a half points.

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:09.410
There's a great temptation there,
isn't it, of some type.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.090
Win four and a half points.

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:14.182
And the coincidence resonated later.

00:38:14.182 --> 00:38:17.790
And, but, you don't win the first point.

00:38:19.470 --> 00:38:23.646
And he win the second point, and
then there's [INAUDIBLE] bit confusion.

00:38:23.646 --> 00:38:28.433
Then win the third point,
panic, and then capitulation.

00:38:28.433 --> 00:38:29.266
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:38:29.266 --> 00:38:31.808
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately, with that message,

00:38:31.808 --> 00:38:35.930
put Europe [INAUDIBLE] west game,
10 six in front.

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:37.580
Fantastic.

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:40.794
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; It's a disease.

00:38:40.794 --> 00:38:41.797
We were playing Everton.

00:38:41.797 --> 00:38:43.177
We lost the league because of it.

00:38:43.177 --> 00:38:45.698
Four to two, seven minutes to go.

00:38:45.698 --> 00:38:47.195
We lost two goals.

00:38:47.195 --> 00:38:51.036
And that causes a weak and
their human beings,

00:38:51.036 --> 00:38:54.900
the best way to keep reminding them.

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.600
That complacency could cost everything.

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:00.960
And there's no way out
when complacency sets in.

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:02.530
There is no way out.

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:03.260
Absolutely no way out.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:10.650
&gt;&gt; One other question from me and then
I know we have some audience questions.

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:16.430
You had this incredible
record that's unparalleled.

00:39:18.040 --> 00:39:21.960
Did you ever feel, or
do you feel that you should have won more?

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:24.180
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:26.580
I do honestly.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:30.940
I think that European cups
was my biggest regret.

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:33.730
And on some occasion were very unlucky.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:39.100
Some occasions where we got
bad decisions against us and

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:41.160
some occasions we didn't play well enough.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:41.885
&gt;&gt; Mm hm.

00:39:41.885 --> 00:39:46.419
&gt;&gt; And the three semi-finals we lost and

00:39:46.419 --> 00:39:51.520
the two were against Dortmund in '97 And

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:55.214
against in 2001.

00:39:55.214 --> 00:39:56.219
We should have won those games.

00:39:56.219 --> 00:39:58.210
&gt;&gt; Does that still bother you today?

00:39:58.210 --> 00:39:59.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it does.

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:04.230
I don't think you should
ever look back with regrets.

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:09.366
But when you ask a question,
that it bothers me all the time,

00:40:09.366 --> 00:40:14.402
and so I, but I have no bitterness
about it cause football,

00:40:14.402 --> 00:40:19.681
I can't work in my career and
[INAUDIBLE] and regret anything.

00:40:19.681 --> 00:40:24.113
I had a fantastic time, but
the question is, the way you put it to me,

00:40:24.113 --> 00:40:27.565
yes we should have won more
European championships.

00:40:27.565 --> 00:40:32.600
Our last season playing
Real Madrid [INAUDIBLE] referee.

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:38.832
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What do you really think of him?

00:40:38.832 --> 00:40:40.830
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:43.510
Absolutely control the game.

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:47.820
And there wasn't a player at that time,

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:50.780
it's ten minutes to get over and
by that time they scored two goals.

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:54.540
And even at two to one down,
was that we made all the chances.

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:56.320
So, It's a disappointment,

00:40:56.320 --> 00:41:01.280
but, as I say, I can't [INAUDIBLE]
I had.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:04.500
&gt;&gt; Let me welcome Nev Given.

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:07.050
She has I know a few
questions from the audience.

00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:12.178
And how many people here are soccer
players in the audience.

00:41:12.178 --> 00:41:13.970
[COUGH] Good.

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:15.096
&gt;&gt; Not bad.

00:41:15.096 --> 00:41:19.011
&gt;&gt; And NCAA soccer players?

00:41:19.011 --> 00:41:20.059
Fantastic.

00:41:20.059 --> 00:41:22.972
&gt;&gt; I'm going to take the liberty
of asking the first question.

00:41:22.972 --> 00:41:25.186
So, you touched on aspects earlier but

00:41:25.186 --> 00:41:29.891
here in the valley our equivalent of the
prize footballers are the best engineers.

00:41:29.891 --> 00:41:34.726
They're paid the most, they get
poached by rival tech companies, yet

00:41:34.726 --> 00:41:38.929
most of us at the GSB are most
likely to go in as their managers.

00:41:38.929 --> 00:41:42.549
For the early days,
what are you top tips to garner their

00:41:42.549 --> 00:41:46.866
respect despite the fact that were
not techie, retain control and

00:41:46.866 --> 00:41:51.893
ensure that no individual becomes larger
than the team or the organization.

00:41:51.893 --> 00:41:56.778
&gt;&gt; We're dealing with
players who are mega-stars or

00:41:56.778 --> 00:42:00.191
players with egos and are wealthy.

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:08.750
I honestly believe that those people
are genuinely the winners all the time.

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:10.830
That's why they go there
in the first place.

00:42:10.830 --> 00:42:15.820
And yeah, there'll be occasions
where they slip off the platform

00:42:16.900 --> 00:42:20.070
of where they were and
maybe get complacent, whatever.

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:24.260
But it's easy to remind
someone who's a winner.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:27.550
It's easy to remind them
how they got there.

00:42:27.550 --> 00:42:29.620
What was their expectation.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:32.090
I think it's important
to remind them of that.

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:34.993
They represent themselves in a big way,
and

00:42:34.993 --> 00:42:38.142
I think that should always
be reminded of them.

00:42:38.142 --> 00:42:42.718
&gt;&gt; What management trait did you
not have that you wish you had and

00:42:42.718 --> 00:42:45.186
how did you compensate for that?

00:42:45.186 --> 00:42:49.977
&gt;&gt; Languages, definitely languages.

00:42:49.977 --> 00:42:55.450
Because when United changed, when
the academy system come into being and

00:42:55.450 --> 00:43:00.315
we had to change the The dynamics
of the club in terms of scouting.

00:43:00.315 --> 00:43:04.868
Then more Europeans players come in,
from South America,

00:43:04.868 --> 00:43:09.650
from France, [INAUDIBLE] to Spain,
whatever, Germany.

00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:13.150
One year, particularly,
there were 21 nationalities in the club.

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:15.560
Now, I had French and German at school.

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:20.394
But, it's like, when you leave school and
you don't use the languages,

00:43:20.394 --> 00:43:21.939
you forget about them.

00:43:21.939 --> 00:43:26.212
So when we start to get, I start
thinking about how I could communicate,

00:43:26.212 --> 00:43:28.607
particularly with the French players.

00:43:28.607 --> 00:43:30.894
And it's not easy.

00:43:30.894 --> 00:43:34.763
Languages is really important.

00:43:34.763 --> 00:43:38.380
I said to my son Mark,
I said years ago when he was in school,

00:43:38.380 --> 00:43:42.520
is another one that went, he was just I'm
going to do this, I'm going to do that.

00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:47.931
[INAUDIBLE],
20 years later.

00:43:47.931 --> 00:43:52.101
It's really important to communicate
with players in their own language.

00:43:52.101 --> 00:43:58.980
That's why I brought and
spoke four, five language.

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:03.030
Fantastic because we had
all these nationalities and

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:05.110
the communication was easy then.

00:44:05.110 --> 00:44:06.820
Carlos would do all that.

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:09.647
And I would do my little
smatterings in French.

00:44:09.647 --> 00:44:16.519
We only had one German player, a young
boy, Ronny Zieler who's with Hannover now.

00:44:16.519 --> 00:44:20.856
He was the only one I needed
to communicate with in German.

00:44:20.856 --> 00:44:23.530
I think that is important, languages.

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:24.250
&gt;&gt; I sympathize with it.

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:26.101
I've barely mastered english.

00:44:26.101 --> 00:44:29.465
I am surprised that weren't more
tweets coming through for racing or

00:44:29.465 --> 00:44:33.184
wine tips from you, seeing that your
track record on that is as good as many.

00:44:33.184 --> 00:44:35.232
&gt;&gt; Forget the racing tips.

00:44:35.232 --> 00:44:39.148
&gt;&gt; I sadly, and
the audience are going to to kill me, but

00:44:39.148 --> 00:44:41.681
we've only got two minutes left.

00:44:41.681 --> 00:44:45.236
So, I'm going to ask a question which,
to you both actually,

00:44:45.236 --> 00:44:47.719
which is somewhat synonymous with the GSB,

00:44:47.719 --> 00:44:51.900
because it's one that we will have to
answer as part of our applications.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:54.607
And that is, what matters most and why?

00:44:54.607 --> 00:44:57.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:44:57.150 --> 00:44:58.899
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:44:58.899 --> 00:45:04.144
&gt;&gt; Why, I think your upbringing.

00:45:04.144 --> 00:45:06.515
I think my upbringing was fantastic.

00:45:06.515 --> 00:45:10.942
I had parents who give me good
disciplines, good advice.

00:45:10.942 --> 00:45:15.150
Don't be late, don't cheat,
don't steal, don't lie.

00:45:15.150 --> 00:45:18.180
These things stood by me all my life.

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:22.616
And I hope, then,
that foundation I bring to me kids, and

00:45:22.616 --> 00:45:24.689
you can bring it to their kids.

00:45:24.689 --> 00:45:27.597
And that's what I'm bringing is,
it's an inheritance.

00:45:27.597 --> 00:45:31.470
That's what it is, and
I think that was important to me.

00:45:31.470 --> 00:45:32.984
&gt;&gt; Best family heirloom.

00:45:32.984 --> 00:45:34.567
Sir Michael?

00:45:34.567 --> 00:45:38.860
&gt;&gt; Following your instincts.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:39.690
&gt;&gt; Short and sweet.

00:45:39.690 --> 00:45:41.189
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, with that,

00:45:41.189 --> 00:45:44.351
please join me in thanking Sir Michael and
Sir Alex.

00:45:44.351 --> 00:45:48.815
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Well done.

00:45:48.815 --> 00:45:54.105
[MUSIC]

